---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { projects, tracks } from "../data/site";
import { cite } from "../lib/bibliography";
import { url } from "../lib/url";
const byId = (id: string) => projects.find((p) => p.id === id)!;
---

<Base>
	<section class="header" id="projects">
		<div class="container">
			<h1>Programming foundations for distributed software</h1>
			<p class="lead">
				Bismuth brings together several research strands of the{" "}
				<a href="https://www.stg.tu-darmstadt.de/">Software Technology Group</a> at TU Darmstadt:{" "}
				<span class="strand track-teal">distributed reactive programming</span>,{" "}
				<span class="strand track-magenta">replicated data types and protocols</span>, and{" "}
				<span class="strand track-orange">trust without a central authority</span>. It contains the Scala
				implementations alongside the papers that formalize them, often with mechanizations in Lean. Bismuth is a continuation
				of <a href="https://www.rescala-lang.com/">REScala</a>.
			</p>
			<div class="summary">
				<div class="summary-card">
					<h3>Abstracted distribution</h3>
					<p>Developers work with ordinary data types and reactive signals. The platform takes care of replication and synchronization.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="summary-card">
					<h3>Local state, global consistency</h3>
					<p>Each device works on its local state and synchronizes when it can. Coordination is only added where an application needs it.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="summary-card">
					<h3>Correct by composition</h3>
					<p>Replicated data types, reactive dataflow, and access control compose. Programs built from them inherit their guarantees.</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<details class="summary-details">
				<summary>Research overview</summary>
				<div class="abstract-body">
				<p>
					Most distributed applications today are built in one of two ways. Either developers write the communication
					themselves, and then also have to work out consistency, fault tolerance, and security for each application. Or
					they use cloud services, which handle these concerns but decide them for the application, and require all data
					to pass through the provider.
				</p>
				<p>
					Bismuth explores a third option. Distribution is handled by programming abstractions that developers already
					know. Replicated data types are ordinary algebraic data types, and the merge of concurrent changes is derived from
					their structure. Reactive signals describe how values depend on each other, and updates propagate through these
					dependencies across devices. Access control and encryption are part of the replicated data itself, so no trusted
					server is needed.
				</p>
				<p>
					Each device works on its local copy and stays usable without a network connection. Changes are synchronized
					when possible and all copies converge to the same state. Where an application needs stronger guarantees, for
					example to keep an invariant like a non-negative balance, coordination is added only for the affected operations.
					Consensus protocols themselves can be built from the same replicated data types.
				</p>
				<p>
					The building blocks are designed to compose. Properties such as convergence or the safety of a protocol are
					proven for each building block, often mechanized in Lean, and then hold for programs built from them. The
					timeline below lists the individual projects and papers.
				</p>
				</div>
			</details>
			<h2 class="timeline-title">Research timeline</h2>
			<div class="timeline">
				{
					projects.map((p) => (
						<article class="project" id={p.id}>
							<div class="year">{p.year}</div>
							<h3>{p.title}</h3>
							<div class="tags">
								{p.tracks.map((t) => <span class:list={["tag", `track-${tracks[t].color}`]}>{tracks[t].title}</span>)}
							</div>
							<p>
								{p.summary}
								{p.links?.map((l) => <> <a href={l.href.startsWith("/") ? url(l.href) : l.href}>{l.label} ↗</a></>)}
							</p>
							{p.related && (
								<p class="related">
									Builds on:{" "}
									{p.related.map((id, i) => (
										<>
											{i > 0 && ", "}
											<a href={`#${id}`} title={byId(id).title}>{byId(id).short}</a>
										</>
									))}
								</p>
							)}
							{p.publications.length > 0 && (
								<ul class="pubs">
									{p.publications.map((pub) => {
										const e = cite(pub.key);
										return (
											<li>
												<span class="venue">{pub.venue}</span> — {e.url ? <a href={e.url}>{e.title}</a> : e.title}
												<div class="authors">
													{e.authors.join(", ")}
													{pub.artifact && <> · <a href={cite(pub.artifact).url}>research artifact</a></>}
												</div>
											</li>
										);
									})}
								</ul>
							)}
						</article>
					))
				}
			</div>
			<p class="all-pubs"><a href={url("/publications/")}>All publications →</a></p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section bg-indigo">
		<div class="container">
			<h2>Get involved</h2>
			<p class="lead">
				Bismuth is developed at the Software Technology Group of TU Darmstadt. Anyone is welcome to join our matrix channel if you want to know more about our research or the Bismuth library.
			</p>
			<div class="buttons">
				<a class="hero" href="https://matrix.to/#/#difosys:matrix.tu-darmstadt.de">Join the chat</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</Base>
